// The Firestore security rule Mealz needs on the shared phil-mini-apps project.
// This is a RECORD of the rule, for reference. The live version lives in the
// Firebase console, which is the only place it takes effect.
//
// Deliberately NO real email addresses in this file. This repo is public, and
// write access is granted by email: listing the family's actual addresses here
// would publish the exact accounts an attacker would need to take over in order
// to edit the plan. The real allowlist lives only in the console. Keep it that
// way when adding Cam's brothers - edit it there, not here.
//
// To apply or change it: Firebase console -> Firestore Database -> Rules, and
// edit the mealz block alongside the blocks already there. Do NOT paste this
// file over the whole ruleset: the other family apps have their own blocks in
// the same document, and replacing everything would switch them off.

// Mealz: the family meal plan. Public read, so anyone can see what is for tea
// without signing in. Only the listed family accounts may change it, and that
// is enforced by Firestore itself, not just hidden in the UI.
    match /mealz/{document} {
      allow read: if true;
      allow write: if request.auth != null
        && request.auth.token.email.lower() in [
          'first.family.member@example.com',
          'second.family.member@example.com'
        ];
    }

// Notes on the shape:
//
//   /mealz/{document}   covers both documents Mealz uses, `plan` and `shopping`.
//   allow read: if true is intentional. The plan is not sensitive, and it means
//     nobody has to sign in just to check what is for tea.
//   .lower() matters: Firebase preserves the case a user typed at sign-up, so
//     comparing without it would refuse a legitimate editor who typed a capital.
//
// If the plan will not load at all and the console shows permission-denied,
// this block is missing or misspelled. If reading works but saving fails, the
// signed-in address is simply not on the allowlist.
